(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["gravity"]="<h1>Gravity, Posture and Acceleration</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-gravity-adds-pressure-below-the-heart-and-takes-it-away-above\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Gravity adds pressure below the heart and takes it away above</h2>\n<p>When you stand, the blood in your vessels forms a tall column. Like water in a tall tank, the pressure is <strong>higher at the bottom</strong> and <strong>lower at the top</strong>. This extra pressure comes from the <strong>weight of the blood</strong>, not from the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>How much?</strong> About <strong>0.77 mmHg for every centimetre</strong> of height (ΔP = ρgh). <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return\">→ Veins and Venous Return</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Standing, with no compensation (the lecture's figures):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Level</th>\n  <th>About how far from the heart</th>\n  <th>Mean arterial pressure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Head</strong></td>\n  <td>45 cm <strong>above</strong></td>\n  <td>About <strong>60</strong> mmHg (about 35 less)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Heart</strong></td>\n  <td>—</td>\n  <td>About <strong>93</strong> mmHg (120/80)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Feet</strong></td>\n  <td>115 cm <strong>below</strong></td>\n  <td>About <strong>180</strong> mmHg (about 90 more)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The same applies to the <strong>veins</strong>: venous pressure in the feet rises to about 90 mmHg, while the veins of the neck collapse.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure gravity-column-figure\" id=\"cvs19-column\"><figcaption>Figure. Pressures in a standing body (no compensation)</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 450\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Outline of a standing person with the heart as the reference level. Mean arterial pressure is about 93 mmHg at the heart, about 60 at the head, 45 centimetres above, and about 180 at the feet, 115 centimetres below. The leg veins are shaded where they stretch and pool about 500 millilitres of blood.\"><defs><marker id=\"gv-head\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"gv-arrowhead\"/></marker></defs><g class=\"gv-body\"><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"40\" r=\"24\"/><rect x=\"142\" y=\"62\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\"/><rect x=\"118\" y=\"74\" width=\"64\" height=\"158\" rx=\"14\"/><rect x=\"100\" y=\"80\" width=\"16\" height=\"130\" rx=\"8\"/><rect x=\"184\" y=\"80\" width=\"16\" height=\"130\" rx=\"8\"/><rect x=\"122\" y=\"228\" width=\"24\" height=\"186\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"154\" y=\"228\" width=\"24\" height=\"186\" rx=\"10\"/><ellipse cx=\"132\" cy=\"418\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"168\" cy=\"418\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"7\"/></g><g class=\"gv-pool\"><rect x=\"124\" y=\"300\" width=\"20\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\"/><rect x=\"156\" y=\"300\" width=\"20\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\"/></g><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"140\" r=\"7\" class=\"gv-heart\"/><line x1=\"92\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"392\" y2=\"140\" class=\"gv-level\"/><g class=\"gv-arrow\"><path d=\"M78 136V46\" marker-end=\"url(#gv-head)\"/><path d=\"M78 144V410\" marker-end=\"url(#gv-head)\"/></g><g text-anchor=\"end\"><text x=\"70\" y=\"86\" class=\"gv-lab\">45 cm</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"104\" class=\"gv-sub gv-down\">−35</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"270\" class=\"gv-lab\">115 cm</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"288\" class=\"gv-sub gv-up\">+90</text></g><text x=\"215\" y=\"34\" class=\"gv-lab\">Head</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"52\" class=\"gv-sub\">≈ 60 mmHg</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"124\" class=\"gv-lab\">Heart level</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"160\" class=\"gv-sub\">≈ 93 mmHg (120/80)</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"330\" class=\"gv-lab\">Leg veins stretch</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"348\" class=\"gv-sub\">≈ 500 mL pools</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"404\" class=\"gv-lab\">Feet</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"422\" class=\"gv-sub\">≈ 180 mmHg</text></svg><p>Mean arterial pressure at each level. Gravity adds about 0.77 mmHg for every centimetre below the heart and removes the same above it. Original DOMiNANCE diagram.</p></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-what-standing-up-does-to-the-circulation\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> What standing up does to the circulation</h2>\n<p><strong>Stand up quickly, and this is what happens in the first few seconds:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The high pressure in the legs <strong>stretches the leg veins</strong>, which are very compliant. About <strong>500 mL</strong> of blood <strong>pools</strong> in them and in the abdominal veins.</li>\n<li>Higher capillary pressure in the legs pushes <strong>fluid out</strong> into the tissues. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema\">→ Oedema</a>)</em></li>\n<li>Less blood returns to the heart, so <strong><span data-term=\"venous-return\">venous return</span></strong> and <strong>end-diastolic volume</strong> fall.</li>\n<li>By the Frank–Starling mechanism, <strong>stroke volume</strong> falls, so <strong>cardiac output</strong> and <strong>blood pressure</strong> fall. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output\">→ Cardiac Output</a>)</em></li>\n<li>Blood pressure at <strong>head level</strong> is already lower because of gravity, so <strong>cerebral blood flow</strong> falls. You may feel light-headed or see the world grey out for a moment.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> How the body rescues blood pressure</h2>\n<p><strong>The baroreflex (seconds).</strong> The carotid sinus and aortic arch sit <strong>above</strong> the heart, so they feel the fall in pressure first. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes?section=3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step\">→ Reflexes, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Baroreceptors <strong>fire less</strong> → the vasomotor centre is released.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic firing rises</strong>, vagal firing falls:<ul>\n<li><strong>Faster heart</strong> (chronotropic) and <strong>stronger beats</strong> (inotropic).</li>\n<li><strong>Arterioles constrict</strong>, raising peripheral resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Veins constrict</strong>, pushing pooled blood back towards the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cardiac output and blood pressure recover.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That is why your <strong>heart rate rises</strong> by 10–20 beats/min when you stand.</p>\n<p><strong>The muscle pump.</strong> Every time the leg muscles contract, they squeeze the deep veins, and the one-way valves send blood <strong>upwards</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return\">→ Veins and Venous Return</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Hormones (minutes to hours).</strong> <strong>Renin</strong>, angiotensin II and <strong>aldosterone</strong> help hold pressure and volume if you stay upright. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/neural-humoral?section=5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation\">→ Neural and Hormonal Control, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<h3>Why the brain is partly protected</h3>\n<p>Standing lowers arterial pressure at head level, but the brain has three extra defences:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Venous pressure in the head falls too.</strong> Cerebral perfusion pressure is the <strong>difference</strong> between arterial and venous pressure, so it falls less than arterial pressure does.</li>\n<li><strong>Intracranial pressure falls</strong>, so there is less pressure squeezing the cerebral vessels. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=4-a-rigid-box-intracranial-pressure-and-perfusion\">→ Cerebral Circulation, §4</a>)</em></li>\n<li>The small fall in flow lets <strong>CO₂ rise and O₂ fall</strong> locally, which <strong>dilates</strong> the cerebral arterioles. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=3-how-does-the-brain-keep-its-blood-flow-steady\">→ Cerebral Circulation, §3</a>)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p>So cerebral blood flow falls only a little.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A person stands with mean arterial pressure of 93 mmHg at heart level. Why is the pressure in the foot arteries about 180 mmHg?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The leg arteries constrict and raise their own pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The weight of the blood column adds pressure below the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart pumps harder to push blood down to the feet</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The leg veins return blood faster, raising arterial pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The extra pressure comes from gravity (about 0.77 mmHg per cm), not from the heart.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does cardiac output fall briefly when you stand up suddenly?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The baroreceptors fire faster and slow the heart down</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The arterioles in the legs dilate and lower peripheral resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Gravity makes the ventricles contract less strongly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood pools in the leg veins, lowering venous return and stroke volume</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Less blood returns to the heart, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume fall (Frank–Starling).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does cerebral blood flow fall only a little on standing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Venous and intracranial pressures fall too, and the arterioles dilate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The Circle of Willis pumps extra blood up to the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Gravity does not affect blood pressure above the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The vertebral arteries constrict to hold blood in the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Perfusion pressure depends on the arterial–venous difference, ICP falls, and local CO₂ dilates the vessels.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> When compensation fails: prolonged standing and fainting</h2>\n<p><strong>Standing still for a long time</strong> (a soldier on parade) defeats these defences:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>With no walking, there is <strong>no muscle pump</strong>.</li>\n<li>Blood keeps <strong>pooling</strong> in the legs, and fluid keeps filtering into the tissues.</li>\n<li>Venous return, cardiac output and cerebral blood flow <strong>fall</strong>.</li>\n<li>The person <strong>faints</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fainting is protective.</strong> Falling flat brings the <strong>head level with the heart</strong>, so gravity no longer drains blood away from the brain, and consciousness returns quickly. Holding a fainted person upright is dangerous.</p>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"vasovagal\">Vasovagal syncope</span> (the common faint).</strong> Strong emotion (fear, the sight of blood), pain, heat or prolonged standing can trigger a sudden <strong>reflex</strong>: sympathetic tone switches <strong>off</strong> (vessels dilate) and vagal tone switches <strong>on</strong> (the heart slows). Blood pressure and cerebral flow drop, and the person faints. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes?section=7-other-inputs-and-the-lecture-s-exceptions\">→ Reflexes, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Notice the difference from ordinary standing: ordinary standing gives a <strong>faster</strong> heart; vasovagal syncope gives a <strong>slower</strong> heart and dilated vessels.</p>\n<p>The lecture lists other types of syncope:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Micturition syncope:</strong> fainting while passing urine, especially after standing up at night.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"carotid-sinus-syncope\">Carotid sinus syncope</span>:</strong> pressure on a sensitive carotid sinus (a <strong>tight collar</strong>, turning the head) triggers a strong baroreflex, with bradycardia and vasodilation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-orthostatic-postural-hypotension\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Orthostatic (postural) hypotension</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"orthostatic-hypotension\">Orthostatic hypotension</span></strong> is a fall in blood pressure on standing that the body fails to correct. It is defined as a sustained fall of at least <strong>20 mmHg systolic</strong> or <strong>10 mmHg diastolic</strong> within <strong>3 minutes</strong> of standing.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does it happen?</strong> Something stops the sympathetic response from working:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"autonomic-neuropathy\">Autonomic neuropathy</span></strong> damages the sympathetic nerves, for example in <strong>diabetes</strong> (or, classically, syphilis).</li>\n<li><strong>Sympatholytic drugs</strong> block the sympathetic response.</li>\n<li><strong>Autonomic failure</strong>, including during and after surgery and anaesthesia.</li>\n<li><strong>Low blood volume</strong> (dehydration, bleeding) leaves less blood to work with.</li>\n<li><strong>Prolonged bed rest</strong> blunts the reflexes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What gives it away?</strong> In a patient with autonomic failure, blood pressure falls on standing but the heart rate <strong>doesn't rise</strong>, and <strong>noradrenaline levels don't rise either</strong>. The baroreceptors detect the fall, but the signal never reaches the vessels.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–5\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–5 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does a soldier standing motionless on parade sometimes faint?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Standing still raises intracranial pressure and compresses the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The baroreceptors fire faster when the body is completely still</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Without the muscle pump, blood pools in the legs and cerebral flow falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Holding the breath for long periods lowers the blood's oxygen content</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Pooling lowers venous return and cardiac output. Fainting brings the head to heart level and restores flow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does vasovagal syncope differ from the normal response to standing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The heart slows and the vessels dilate, instead of speeding up and constricting</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The heart speeds up and the vessels constrict, as in normal standing</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Only the heart rate changes, while the vessels stay the same</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood pressure rises suddenly, and the high pressure causes fainting</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> In vasovagal syncope, sympathetic tone is withdrawn and vagal tone rises.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A man with diabetic autonomic neuropathy feels dizzy on standing. His blood pressure falls but his heart rate stays at 72. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. His baroreceptors fire faster when he stands, slowing his heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Damaged sympathetic nerves can't raise heart rate or constrict vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. His blood volume is too high for the heart rate to rise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. His vagus nerve has been cut, so his heart rate is fixed</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The baroreceptors detect the fall, but the sympathetic pathway to the heart and vessels is damaged.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Acceleration: when “gravity” gets stronger</h2>\n<p>In a fast aircraft or a roller coaster, acceleration acts like <strong>stronger gravity</strong>. It is measured in <strong>g</strong> (1 g = normal gravity).</p>\n<p><strong>Positive g (+g): blood is thrown towards the feet.</strong> For example, pulling out of a dive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood pools in the legs and abdomen; head-level pressure falls.</li>\n<li>At first, the same defences as standing help: venous and intracranial pressures fall, and sympathetic drive raises heart rate and contractility. Cardiac output is held for a while.</li>\n<li>Above about <strong>5 g</strong>, cerebral perfusion fails: <strong>vision fails first</strong> (&quot;grey-out&quot;), then <strong>blackout</strong>, then <strong>unconsciousness</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why does vision fail first?</strong> The retina is supplied at head level by arteries that must push against the pressure inside the eye. When arterial pressure at head level falls, retinal flow is the first to stop.</p>\n<p><strong>Negative g (−g): blood is thrown towards the head.</strong> For example, an outside loop.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arterial and venous pressures in the head <strong>rise</strong>.</li>\n<li>The head and neck vessels <strong>congest</strong>: bleeding around the eyes, a <strong>throbbing headache</strong>, mental confusion and reddened vision (&quot;<strong>red-out</strong>&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Why don't the brain vessels burst?</strong> Intracranial pressure rises <strong>at the same time</strong> (from the venous congestion), so the pressure outside the vessels rises with the pressure inside. The <strong><span data-term=\"transmural-pressure\">transmural pressure</span></strong> stays small. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation?section=3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic\">→ Local Regulation, §3</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How pilots cope with +g:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"anti-g-suit\">Anti-g suits</span></strong> inflate around the <strong>abdomen and legs</strong>, squeezing them with a force matched to the g. This reduces pooling and raises venous return.</li>\n<li>Lying <strong>horizontal</strong> (as astronauts do during launch) makes the blood column across the body short, so much higher g is tolerated.</li>\n<li>g is tolerated better when it acts <strong>across</strong> the body rather than along it.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Section 6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Section 6 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Section 6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A pilot pulling out of a dive at 6 g loses vision before losing consciousness. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The retina needs less oxygen than the rest of the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Blood is thrown towards the head, congesting the eyes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The intraocular pressure falls, letting the retina detach</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Retinal flow stops first as head-level arterial pressure falls</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> At +g, blood moves to the feet. Retinal arteries must overcome the pressure in the eye, so their flow fails first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> In negative g, why don't the cerebral vessels rupture despite the high pressure inside them?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The cerebral arteries constrict strongly enough to resist any pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Blood is thrown towards the feet, emptying the cerebral vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Intracranial pressure rises too, keeping the pressure across the wall small</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The Circle of Willis diverts the extra blood away from the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Pressure outside the vessels rises with the pressure inside, so the transmural pressure stays low.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> How does an anti-g suit protect a pilot?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It squeezes the head and neck, stopping blood from leaving the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It squeezes the abdomen and legs, reducing pooling and raising venous return</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It warms the body, dilating the cerebral arterioles to raise flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It raises oxygen pressure in the lungs to protect the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Compressing the lower body stops blood pooling there, so venous return and cardiac output are maintained.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Gravity</strong> adds about <strong>0.77 mmHg per cm</strong> below the heart and removes it above. Standing: about <strong>60</strong> mmHg at the head, <strong>93</strong> at the heart, <strong>180</strong> at the feet.</li>\n<li><strong>Standing</strong> → about 500 mL pools in the legs → venous return, stroke volume and cardiac output fall → cerebral flow falls.</li>\n<li><strong>Rescue:</strong> baroreflex (faster, stronger heart; arterioles and veins constrict), <strong>muscle pump</strong>, then renin–angiotensin–aldosterone.</li>\n<li><strong>The brain is protected</strong> because venous pressure and ICP fall too, and local CO₂ dilates its arterioles.</li>\n<li><strong>Prolonged standing</strong> without the muscle pump → pooling → fainting, which restores flow by bringing the head to heart level.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasovagal syncope:</strong> sympathetic withdrawal + vagal activation → dilation and <strong>bradycardia</strong>. Also micturition and carotid sinus syncope.</li>\n<li><strong>Orthostatic hypotension:</strong> fall of ≥20 systolic or ≥10 diastolic within 3 min. Causes: autonomic neuropathy (diabetes), drugs, surgery, low volume. Heart rate and noradrenaline fail to rise.</li>\n<li><strong>+g:</strong> blood to the feet → grey-out, blackout, unconsciousness above about 5 g. <strong>−g:</strong> blood to the head → congestion, red-out; vessels protected by the rise in ICP.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-g suits</strong> compress the abdomen and legs; <strong>horizontal</strong> positioning raises tolerance.</li>\n</ol>\n";
